<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Longmen Grottoes</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Longmen Grottoes are Buddhist cave temples cut into limestone cliffs on both banks of the Yi river south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henan">Henan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Begun around 493 under the Northern Wei, after the court moved to Luoyang, they were extended under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang dynasty</a> and contain about 100,000 statues, including the colossal Vairocana Buddha of the Fengxian temple, completed in 675. They are a World Heritage Site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/longmen-grottoes.jpg" alt="Longmen Grottoes" width="320" height="201" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: xiquinhosilva · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:27427-Luoyang_(49067744628).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
